From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qwqgong-ui <noreply@qwqgong-ui.local>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 17:09:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] tun: skip the redundant GRO checksum validation on the
 write path

coalesceTCPPackets and coalesceUDPPackets validate the L4 checksum of both
the head and the incoming packet before merging them, which is a full pass
over the payload for every packet that gets coalesced. On the tun write path
the packets are ours: the system stack has just recomputed the checksum
after its NAT rewrite, or a packet writer encoded a fresh header over
proxied payload. Thread a trustCSum flag through handleGRO and let the
device write path set it.

Besides removing a second checksum pass per packet, this keeps coalescing
available when _TXChecksumOffload zeroes the checksum on purpose: the
validation would fail on the zero and quietly turn every TSO/USO batch back
into one write per segment.
---
 tun_linux.go         |  9 ++++++++-
 tun_offload_linux.go | 34 +++++++++++++++++-----------------
 2 files changed, 25 insertions(+), 18 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tun_linux.go b/tun_linux.go
index dac98e5..a2fc053 100644
--- a/tun_linux.go
+++ b/tun_linux.go
@@ -233,7 +233,14 @@ func (t *NativeTun) BatchWrite(buffers [][]byte, offset int) (int, error) {
 	)
 	t.gsoToWrite = t.gsoToWrite[:0]
 	if t.vnetHdr {
-		err := handleGRO(buffers, offset, t.tcpGROTable, t.udpGROTable, t.gro, &t.gsoToWrite)
+		// Every buffer reaching BatchWrite was built by this process: either
+		// the system stack rewrote a packet and recomputed its checksum, or a
+		// packet writer encoded a fresh header over proxied payload. Re-summing
+		// the whole payload here only to compare it against a checksum we just
+		// wrote is a second full pass over the data, and with
+		// _TXChecksumOffload the deliberately zeroed checksum would fail the
+		// check outright and silently disable coalescing.
+		err := handleGRO(buffers, offset, t.tcpGROTable, t.udpGROTable, t.gro, true, &t.gsoToWrite)
 		if err != nil {
 			return 0, err
 		}
